{"purpose":"Define file movement between devices for the development and troubleshooting use cases, where transfers happen over links that are expected to be interrupted.","content":"## Requirement\n\nA device must be able to send a file to another device, see progress while it transfers, and resume an interrupted transfer without resending what already arrived.\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- **Resume is about interruption, not packet loss.** The path between devices is already reliable; what fails is the session — a laptop closing, a link dropping, a process restarting. Resume must survive the receiver's process exiting and starting again.\n- **Integrity is verified before the file is usable.** The received data is written under a temporary name and only exposed under its final name after whole-file verification succeeds. A partially written file must never appear as a complete one.\n- **Progress reflects acknowledged bytes**, not bytes handed to the network. A progress bar that runs ahead of reality is a bug.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A file transferred between two online devices arrives byte-identical, verified by hash.\n2. Killing the receiver mid-transfer and restarting it resumes from what was already received, without resending completed portions.\n3. A transfer interrupted and never resumed leaves no file under the final name.\n4. Corrupted or truncated data is detected and reported rather than silently accepted.\n5. Progress reported to the user never exceeds what the receiver has acknowledged."}